# Blocks: every exercise in the book.
#
# Chapter 1 is procedures over numbers and nothing else — the definition of a library. Beck
# compiles one happily and cannot run one, so an exercise's own answer cannot be asserted without
# an application wrapped around it. `sicp/ch1.beck` carries that wrapper, five declarations of it,
# used by nothing.
#
# `beck check` on this file: ok, "a library. No merge point, so there is nothing to run".
# `beck test` on this file: error[B0500] — "this program has no merge point".
#
# Known: docs/22 §22.6 — "a library module … cannot have its tests run … a real gap for exactly
# the modules that most want unit tests." The SICP suite is 356 exercises' worth of exactly those
# modules, which is why the report puts this first.

def factorial(n: Int) -> Int:
    if n == 0:
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n - 1)

test "1.2.1 — factorial":
    expect factorial(6) == 720
